## Spacing scale

Use a 5px base scale: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 40, 50 and 60px. Use 15px or 20px inside a card, 20px or 30px between related groups and 40px or more between page sections. The aim is a consistent relationship between elements. It is not a rule to override every native padding value in Home Assistant.

## Dashboard layout

Use Home Assistant’s native responsive sections and grid. Group cards by task or room, align them and use headings to name each group. Do not put unrelated heating, security, energy and media controls in one card to fill space.

Do not give text cards a fixed height. Use a single column when the width needs it. Do not shrink a desktop dashboard into unreadable miniatures on a phone.

## Surfaces and borders

Use spacing, headings and flat surfaces to group content. Add a quiet 1px border only where a group needs an edge. Do not nest boxes around a card, its sections and every row. A container is not an input, so keep their borders different.

| Role | Light mode | Dark mode | Use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Structural divider | `#CECECE` | `#50565A` | Card edges, row separators and section rules, normally 1px |
| Essential control outline | `#6F777B` | `#9BA4AA` | An outline that identifies an interactive control |
| Editable field boundary | `#0B0C0C` | White | Strong input boundary, normally 2px |
| Focus | Yellow with ink | Yellow with ink, or the documented native treatment | The current keyboard target, never decoration |

A decorative divider does not need the contrast of a control boundary. Do not weaken an input boundary or a focus indicator to make a dense page look quieter.

Use square corners on maintained cards, buttons, fields and dialogs. Do not set every Home Assistant radius to zero. [Notification badges](../components/notification-badge.html), radio controls, switches, avatars and gauges need their own shapes. Avoid glow and large ambient shadows. A restrained bottom edge on a button may show that it can be pressed.

## Responsive behaviour

Test at 320 CSS pixels, at 200% text zoom and at 400% page zoom. Navigation may wrap or collapse using its native behaviour. A table or a truly two-dimensional chart may scroll inside its own labelled region. The whole page must not scroll sideways by accident.

Do not lock body scrolling or hide native navigation to make a screenshot look cleaner. Keep the person’s way back to Home Assistant settings and dashboards.

## Density

A home dashboard can be denser than a one-task form, because people use it repeatedly. Keep labels readable and keep controls in maintained custom UI at least 44px. Do not shrink text or targets to fit more cards.

## Dashboard canvas

Use a plain canvas behind controls and reading content. Photographs and animated scenes compete with the hierarchy and make contrast unpredictable. Keep such imagery inside a purposeful component or a separately chosen immersive view. Set up background integrations with their own controls. Do not hide the whole application or remove weather data.
